opennyai_summary.arguments:
15. The learned counsel for the plaintiff contended as follows:
i) that the First Appellate Court failed to note that the land in Sy.No.211 measuring 32 guntas of Bommasandra village was already acquired by defendant No.2 in terms of a preliminary notification dated 28.02.1981 followed by a final notification and the possession of the property was taken and an award was passed in the year 1987. That the defendant No.1 had already formed an industrial area comprising several plots.
That the non-party defendant claimed that plot No.253 which was allotted to plaintiff included the land in Sy.No.211 measuring 34 guntas;
ii) That when the plot No.253 was allotted to the plaintiff and possession certificate was handed over and a lease cum sale agreement was executed in favour of the plaintiff, it demonstrated that the land was acquired and a layout was formed and therefore, the non-party defendant did not get any title in respect of the said propert;
iii) That though the plaintiff had complied with the terms and conditions of lease cum sale agreement, the defendant No.1 failed to execute the sale deed, but issued a notice dated 07.04.2005 alleging that it had violated the terms of lease cum sale agreement.
In this regard he relied upon the following judgments:
i) Shri Hoshiar Singh Negi Vs. Shri Durga Dass Bedi (deceased) Through his Legal Heir Shri Baldev Bedi - RSA No.85/2008 decided on 12.05.2011;
ii) Ramesh Chand Ardawatiya Vs. Anil  Panjwani - AIR 2003 SC 2508;
iii) Shivshankara and Another Vs.
 H.P.Vedavyasa char - AIR 2023 SC 1780 vi) That in a Miscellaneous Second Appeal the High Court can only examine the legality of the order of the remand and if the Court comes to a conclusion that the order is bad, then it could only remand the case to the First Appellate Court to decide the appeal on merits;
vii) He contended that the suit property in O.S.No.1232/2006 is a public premises, as it stood resumed to the defendant No.1 and therefore, a suit is not maintainable under Section 25 of the Karnataka Industrial Areas Development Act, 1966 (henceforth referred to as 'KIADB Act' for short) and the jurisdiction of Civil Court is excluded under Section 45 of KIADB Act;
viii) That the decree granted by the Trial Court in O.S.No.1232/2006 bound only the parties to the suit and not third parties.
That the plaintiff under the garb of the decree in O.S.No.1232/2006 was trying to recover possession of Sy.No.211 from the non-party defendant;
ix) That since the lease cum sale agreement is cancelled and the possession of the site is resumed by KIADB, the non-party defendant as well as the plaintiff had no title and if the plaintiff wanted to take possession, he had to first establish his title as provided under Section 6(4) of Specific Relief Act, 1964.
He contended that an appeal was preferred by him under Section 136 of the Karnataka Land Revenue Act, 1964 who passed an order restoring the name of the non-party defendant in Column No.9 of the RTC and the right of KIABD in column No.11.
